  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1150 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAnother warm and humid day is in store for today, under the\nnorthern portion of a 593dam 500mb ridge. A convective corridor\nwill extend from southern MO into the lower OH River Valley\nduring the pre-dawn hours, where the stronger westerlies prevail.\nA few storms will likely propagate southeast, aided by outflow\ntoward midmorning. 00Z CAMs showed a rather wide variation of\nconvective scenarios with respect to storm timing and coverage\nfor the Midsouth later today. Taken together, ensemble solutions\ncontinued to point toward thunderstorm chances for areas mainly\nnorth of I-40. Where storms don't significantly modify\ntemperatures, mid 70s dewpoints will help drive afternoon heat\nindices again to the mid to upper 90s.\n\nStorms chances should diminish by early evening, as the boundary\nlayer stabilizes. Concurrently, midlevel heights will build\nslightly tonight, shifting the storm track north. Drier surface\nair over central MS today will spread north into the Midsouth on\nThursday, with dewpoints back down in the upper 50s to low 60s.\nHighs will top out near 90 over most of the Midsouth Thursday\nafternoon, our last summer-like day before steep northern branch\nheight falls drive a strong cold front through the Midsouth early\nFriday. With limited pre-frontal moisture, this front will pass\nthrough dry. Despite much cooler post-frontal temperatures, a\nlarger dewpoint drop will bring relative humidity down to as low\nas 35 percent percent Friday afternoon. Unlike our recent low\nhumidity days, Friday will bring strong northwest winds which\nwill elevate wildland fire danger.\n\nThe Canadian airmass will settle into the Midsouth this weekend,\nwith light winds expected. Overnight lows will drop into the 40s,\nwith upper 30s forecast for northern portions of West Tennessee\nSunday morning.",
